{
  "fromId": "p",
  "toId": "f",
  "preamble": "As part of Grimm's Law, Proto-Indo-European voiceless plosives became voiceless fricatives in Proto-Germanic.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fricativization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Vietnamese (Historical)",
      "languageFamily": "Austroasiatic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Viet-Muong *p",
          "to": "[f] (written ph)",
          "note": "Historical fricativization of initial *p. E.g., Proto-Vietic *pa → Vietnamese 'ba' [ɓa] (three) - wait, ba is b. 'phi' [fi] (to fly) from Middle Chinese or similar."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Tok Pisin",
      "languageFamily": "English-based Creole",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "sapos [p]",
          "to": "[ˈsafos]",
          "note": "Free variation: /p/ and /f/ are often in free variation or act as allophones for many speakers (e.g., 'sapos' ~ 'safos' meaning 'if')."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Biblical Hebrew",
      "languageFamily": "Semitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "פָּרַשׁ pārash [p] (horseman, word-initial)",
          "to": "[ɸ]/[f] in post-vocalic position",
          "note": "Beged-Kefet spirantization: pe (פ) is realized as [p] word-initially or after a consonant, and as [f] (or bilabial [ɸ]) after a vowel. Marked by dagesh: פּ [p] vs. פ [f]. The same root appears in Arabic as فَارِس fāris with /f/ in all positions, showing Arabic generalized the fricative while Hebrew preserves the allophonic alternation. Tiberian Hebrew pronunciation (c. 7th–10th century CE)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Arabic",
      "languageFamily": "Central Semitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *pataḥ- 'to open'",
          "to": "فَتَحَ fataḥa 'to open'",
          "note": "Contrast Hebrew pāṯaḥ and Akkadian petûm with p."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *napš- 'soul, breath'",
          "to": "نَفْس nafs 'soul'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *kapp- 'palm of the hand'",
          "to": "كَفّ kaff 'palm'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *ʔanp- 'nose'",
          "to": "أَنْف ʔanf 'nose'",
          "note": "Arabic has /f/ for Proto-Semitic *p in all positions and no /p/ phoneme."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
